WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.919 --> 00:00:05.690
PATEL: All right, welcome, everyone.

00:00:05.690 --> 00:00:08.000
Thank you for coming.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:13.720
Today we're having a Council meeting on U.S.
economic competitiveness.

00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:17.529
And I'd love to leap right into this conversation,
but just wanted to remind everyone that this

00:00:17.529 --> 00:00:23.720
meeting is on the record and is being livestreamed
on CFR.org.

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:27.000
Today we have three distinguished panelists.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:31.789
Governor John Engler is the president of the
Business Roundtable, which is an association

00:00:31.789 --> 00:00:38.149
of chief executive officers from leading U.S.
corporations that have produced $7.4 trillion

00:00:38.149 --> 00:00:41.609
in annual revenue and employ over 16 million
people.

00:00:41.609 --> 00:00:47.049
And he's a former three-term governor of Michigan,
and previously served as the president and

00:00:47.049 --> 00:00:50.420
CEO of the National Association of Manufacturers.

00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:55.769
Steve Rattner is the chairman of Willett Advisors,
which is an investment arm of Mayor Bloomberg's

00:00:55.769 --> 00:00:58.120
private and philanthropic assets.

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:02.739
And he previously served as a counselor to
the secretary of the treasury, and led the

00:01:02.739 --> 00:01:07.880
Obama administration's successful effort to
restructure the auto industry.

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:12.670
And Jay Shambaugh is a member of the Council
of Economic Advisers at the White House.

00:01:12.670 --> 00:01:17.590
And he's currently on leave from George Washington
University, where he's a professor of economics

00:01:17.590 --> 00:01:19.479
and international affairs.

00:01:19.479 --> 00:01:23.729
These issues have become prominent in the
election debates as of late, both in the United

00:01:23.729 --> 00:01:26.060
States and around the world.

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:31.960
And CEO-GE CEO Jeff Immelt actually recently
laid out the challenges, saying big companies

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:37.759
are distrusted, governments and global institutions
are failing to address the world's challenges,

00:01:37.759 --> 00:01:41.149
and globalization is being attacked as never
before.

00:01:41.149 --> 00:01:45.439
The Council's been focused on this through
the Renewing America initiative, which reviews

00:01:45.439 --> 00:01:51.780
the role of government in creating conditions
for a more competitive economy through education,

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:56.010
workforce retraining, infrastructure, tax
reform, and technological innovation.

00:01:56.010 --> 00:02:00.389
And we actually have the author of the latest
book out of the Council, Ted Alden, here with

00:02:00.389 --> 00:02:04.090
us today, called "How America Stacks Up."

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:06.039
And copies of the book are available outside.

00:02:06.039 --> 00:02:10.750
So to set the stage, let's approach competitiveness
through two frames.

00:02:10.750 --> 00:02:15.080
I think there's the ability for companies
to compete in the globalized economy, and

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:20.060
a second way to think about competitiveness
is the frame of nations, and in particular

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:24.000
the competitive advantage of the United States
as a business location.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:28.690
So what makes the U.S. an attractive investment
for established companies?

00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:34.060
And how can we all better create conditions
to build a resilient and productive economy,

00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:39.120
which will lead to better jobs and opportunities
for the next generation?

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:41.110
Governor Engler, I'd like to start with you.

00:02:41.110 --> 00:02:46.000
If you could share with us, I would love your
perspective on some of the competitive challenges

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:52.489
that are currently facing U.S. manufacturers-whether
it's the dollar, worker skill levels, regulation-and

00:02:52.489 --> 00:02:57.750
in particular what you see and what BRT's
companies would need to make the U.S. a better

00:02:57.750 --> 00:02:58.750
place to invest.

00:02:58.750 --> 00:02:59.890
ENGLER: Well, thank you, Mira.

00:02:59.890 --> 00:03:04.730
I think the number-one thing that Roundtable
member companies would do if we could sort

00:03:04.730 --> 00:03:08.760
of start ticking off the priorities would
be to first change the tax code.

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:14.640
We haven't adjusted the U.S. tax code since
the 1980s, when we have a bipartisan agreement

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:21.519
that came out of Congress and at that time
gave us a highly competitive tax code, a corporate

00:03:21.519 --> 00:03:25.769
tax rate lower than the OECD nations by a
significant amount.

00:03:25.769 --> 00:03:30.510
Also in the '80s, we were just a few years
into an R&amp;D tax credit, which at the beginning

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:32.469
of the '80s was the best in the world.

00:03:32.469 --> 00:03:36.910
That today is, while now permanent thanks
to the work of the Congress and the president

00:03:36.910 --> 00:03:42.969
at the end of last year, it still has diminished
in its impact down to probably-I haven't seen

00:03:42.969 --> 00:03:50.540
the latest rankings-25, 26 in terms of its
efficacy and in terms of impacting R&amp;D.

00:03:50.540 --> 00:03:55.790
But the key thing is that we have more than
$2 trillion trapped offshore that you'd like

00:03:55.790 --> 00:03:56.890
to bring home.

00:03:56.890 --> 00:04:03.790
We are unique among the industrialized nations
in having a tax policy which directs you to

00:04:03.790 --> 00:04:05.450
keep your cash offshore.

00:04:05.450 --> 00:04:08.890
If you're a German company and you earn money
in the U.S., you pay your U.S. tax and you

00:04:08.890 --> 00:04:12.349
can take the money home or anywhere else in
the world.

00:04:12.349 --> 00:04:16.790
If you're a U.S. company and you earn money
in Germany, you pay your tax in Germany, you

00:04:16.790 --> 00:04:19.380
can take it anywhere in the world except the
U.S.

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:24.330
If you bring it back to the U.S., the Treasury's
waiting with their hand out, wanting the differential

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:28.260
on taxes, and that differential will vary
from company to company.

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:33.300
So you'd fix the ability to bring foreign
earnings home, you get the corporate rate

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:38.010
competitive, and this could be done at a relatively
modest cost.

00:04:38.010 --> 00:04:43.370
We've looked at-the corporate piece is not
hard from a technical standpoint; it's hard

00:04:43.370 --> 00:04:47.030
politically because large corporations aren't
in favor.

00:04:47.030 --> 00:04:52.470
You see them being sort of pilloried on all
sides during this particular presidential

00:04:52.470 --> 00:04:53.610
year.

00:04:53.610 --> 00:05:00.970
So that by itself probably can't get across
the finish line, and there's been, I think,

00:05:00.970 --> 00:05:06.540
a disposition at times to use that for different
political purposes rather than just fixing

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:07.540
the problem.

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:09.240
But that is a key competitiveness.

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:15.600
I'd put right behind it, then, fixing immigration
and fixing trade, and some of the other agenda

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:20.130
items which we think all add up to competitiveness.

00:05:20.130 --> 00:05:25.920
It's interesting that immigration is-you know,
taxes and trade are part of what I'd call

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:29.980
capital, but immigration is that human capital.

00:05:29.980 --> 00:05:34.260
And today-and you mentioned it in your opening
comments-workforce and talent.

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:39.380
There's a global competition for talent, and
we aren't winning that one either, so.

00:05:39.380 --> 00:05:46.130
PATEL: Steve, I would love to build on that,
that trade issue.

00:05:46.130 --> 00:05:51.720
Based on your experience with the auto industry,
how do you view trade and, in particular,

00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:55.150
the import competition that that creates for
U.S. workers?

00:05:55.150 --> 00:06:01.440
RATTNER: Look, I think-I think the clich�
about trade is an accurate clich�, which

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:07.350
is that it-properly done, it confers enormous
benefits on the countries that participate

00:06:07.350 --> 00:06:13.770
in it as a whole, and brings with it significant
negative consequences for people who are adversely

00:06:13.770 --> 00:06:18.050
affected by the changing mix of where things-where
things get made.

00:06:18.050 --> 00:06:22.230
And I would just say from my own personal
experience, I've spent my whole career in

00:06:22.230 --> 00:06:25.860
services, mostly working with media and telecoms
companies, and companies that are really not

00:06:25.860 --> 00:06:27.450
part of what we call the tradeable sector.

00:06:27.450 --> 00:06:30.750
And so, when I got involved with autos, it
was a real eye-opener to me in many, many

00:06:30.750 --> 00:06:31.750
respects.

00:06:31.750 --> 00:06:34.610
And I could sort of spend the rest of the
hour talking about it, which nobody wants

00:06:34.610 --> 00:06:35.610
me to do.

00:06:35.610 --> 00:06:42.350
But one of the things that really-that really
was a shock to me is the extent to which other

00:06:42.350 --> 00:06:47.800
companies-other countries, rather, have developed
the expertise to produce the same things we

00:06:47.800 --> 00:06:52.920
produce just as well as we produce them, in
terms of efficiency of labor and so on, but

00:06:52.920 --> 00:06:54.160
at much lower labor costs.

00:06:54.160 --> 00:06:57.430
And I can give you some numbers that are now
somewhat out of date, but go back to when

00:06:57.430 --> 00:06:58.540
we were doing all this.

00:06:58.540 --> 00:07:03.010
But a typical O-line (ph)-when I say O-line
(ph), I mean paid under the traditional compensation

00:07:03.010 --> 00:07:08.750
arrangements-worker at GM or Ford or Chrysler
made about-before we restructured it, made

00:07:08.750 --> 00:07:14.750
about $65 an hour all in, about half cash
wages and about half benefits.

00:07:14.750 --> 00:07:21.350
The non-unionized workers in the South, principally-although
we introduced some lower wage points among

00:07:21.350 --> 00:07:28.230
the big three-made about half of that, also
about half in cash and half in non-cash benefits.

00:07:28.230 --> 00:07:34.570
GM-not some other company, but GM-paid its
workers in Mexico $7 1/2 an hour, it paid

00:07:34.570 --> 00:07:39.080
its workers in China $4 1/2 an hour, and it
paid its workers in India a dollar an hour.

00:07:39.080 --> 00:07:43.950
And I've been to plants in China, and I've
looked at the numbers in Mexico, and the fact

00:07:43.950 --> 00:07:48.650
is-and GM will tell you-that their productivity
from those plants is just as high, if not

00:07:48.650 --> 00:07:50.730
higher in some cases, than it is here.

00:07:50.730 --> 00:07:56.130
And so it's not a shock that if you look at
the output from car factories in the U.S.

00:07:56.130 --> 00:08:00.330
versus Mexico over the last any period of
time, but five, six, seven years, it's gone

00:08:00.330 --> 00:08:04.420
up here, our industry has recovered, but it's
gone up much, much faster, and hiring has

00:08:04.420 --> 00:08:06.900
gone up much, much faster, in Mexico.

00:08:06.900 --> 00:08:09.770
And so that's just a reality of life.

00:08:09.770 --> 00:08:12.770
And I don't have-there's no magic bullet to
change that.

00:08:12.770 --> 00:08:16.270
But what I think we-I think what we have to
do is recognize a couple of things.

00:08:16.270 --> 00:08:19.670
One, that there are some sectors in which
we're going to be able to compete more effectively

00:08:19.670 --> 00:08:20.670
than others.

00:08:20.670 --> 00:08:27.150
And those, in my mind, would be ones with
high skill qualities, high intellectual content.

00:08:27.150 --> 00:08:31.890
But even there, for example, they're now assembling
Learjets in Mexico and CitationJets in China,

00:08:31.890 --> 00:08:35.490
again, things that we never thought probably
were going to happen.

00:08:35.490 --> 00:08:38.260
And secondly-so I think we have to recognize
what we're good at and what we're not good

00:08:38.260 --> 00:08:39.260
at.

00:08:39.260 --> 00:08:42.719
I think we have to do-I think we have to make
sure we do the right things for the people

00:08:42.719 --> 00:08:43.719
who are left behind.

00:08:43.719 --> 00:08:48.340
And I think there, notwithstanding some very
constructive proposals by the Obama administration

00:08:48.340 --> 00:08:53.560
and some constructive steps, which we can
talk about, I think we have done far less

00:08:53.560 --> 00:08:59.670
for these workers that are displaced than
is-than we should have.

00:08:59.670 --> 00:09:03.410
And I think we can see the consequences of
that not only in the wage figures-and you

00:09:03.410 --> 00:09:08.180
look at what's happening to wages of people
in the manufacturing sector in this country,

00:09:08.180 --> 00:09:12.399
which have been going down in real terms pretty
consistently-but you can also see it in our

00:09:12.399 --> 00:09:13.399
politics.

00:09:13.399 --> 00:09:15.649
And I don't think I probably need to say any
more about what I mean by all that.

00:09:15.649 --> 00:09:17.490
I think you can imagine it.

00:09:17.490 --> 00:09:22.450
So I think trade-I think trade is an important
part of our future.

00:09:22.450 --> 00:09:25.649
I think we have to be realistic about what
we can achieve in manufacturing.

00:09:25.649 --> 00:09:29.660
I think we have to recognize that not all
trade are things you make.

00:09:29.660 --> 00:09:32.930
Education can be trade, if somebody comes
here from overseas to be educated.

00:09:32.930 --> 00:09:37.120
Health care can be trade, if somebody comes
here from overseas to get treated at the Cleveland

00:09:37.120 --> 00:09:38.120
Clinic.

00:09:38.120 --> 00:09:43.990
And not try to create some unrealistic idea
of what-of what the job composition of this

00:09:43.990 --> 00:09:45.829
country can look like going forward.

00:09:45.829 --> 00:09:51.200
PATEL: Jay, do you want to respond, to the
perspective of the president and the administration

00:09:51.200 --> 00:09:55.439
on this issue of competitiveness and what
the White House is thinking in terms of bolstering

00:09:55.439 --> 00:09:57.160
our strength in that area?

00:09:57.160 --> 00:09:58.160
SHAMBAUGH: Yeah.

00:09:58.160 --> 00:09:59.160
I mean, I think when-it's funny.

00:09:59.160 --> 00:10:03.620
For me, especially, outside of my role right
now at the White House but as an academic,

00:10:03.620 --> 00:10:07.470
the word "competitiveness" is always a little
odd, I think, to international economists,

00:10:07.470 --> 00:10:12.910
because it seems to set the global economy
up as very zero-sum as opposed to something

00:10:12.910 --> 00:10:16.399
where you think it benefits to all.

00:10:16.399 --> 00:10:20.810
And so I often think of it-and I think Steve
just pointed out, talking about productivity-as

00:10:20.810 --> 00:10:24.269
more a game of productivity, of what are we
doing to make the economy more productive.

00:10:24.269 --> 00:10:29.459
And in that sense, it's not that we're trying
to be more productive than Canada in any sense.

00:10:29.459 --> 00:10:32.050
It's not that we care, looking across it,
too.

00:10:32.050 --> 00:10:35.410
It's we want to be more productive because
being more productive is how you lift living

00:10:35.410 --> 00:10:37.480
standards over time.

00:10:37.480 --> 00:10:41.120
And so, in that sense, I think it goes very
quickly to the questions of what can you do

00:10:41.120 --> 00:10:42.120
to do that.

00:10:42.120 --> 00:10:46.449
And, you know, there are things in education
you can do that lift skills, that make people

00:10:46.449 --> 00:10:47.649
more productive.

00:10:47.649 --> 00:10:52.050
When I look at the president's proposal for
community college for all, if you look back

00:10:52.050 --> 00:10:56.660
at economic history, the U.S. was the first
place to do public education widely, the first

00:10:56.660 --> 00:11:01.079
place to do-to try to push people all the
way through high school widely, and we had

00:11:01.079 --> 00:11:03.329
the best-educated workforce in the world.

00:11:03.329 --> 00:11:06.320
And we've kind of quit pushing on that lens.

00:11:06.320 --> 00:11:10.410
And if you got everybody into at least two
years after high school, that would be the

00:11:10.410 --> 00:11:15.480
kind of thing that would actually fairly quickly
lead to a more-educated workforce and to higher

00:11:15.480 --> 00:11:18.829
productivity, because you'd had that higher
labor quality.

00:11:18.829 --> 00:11:21.870
You've also got areas where you can do more
on innovation.

00:11:21.870 --> 00:11:26.279
I think business tax reform is a big spot
that you could try to make sure that you're

00:11:26.279 --> 00:11:30.999
doing enough to have the right incentives
for R&amp;D, because that's such a crucial part

00:11:30.999 --> 00:11:36.230
of improving innovation and things like that.

00:11:36.230 --> 00:11:39.889
Whether it's also-you can have basic research
that's being funded by the government to try

00:11:39.889 --> 00:11:44.290
to do that, or it could be patent reform and
things like that.

00:11:44.290 --> 00:11:49.050
I think, personally, some of the stuff we've
been doing recently on competition-not competition

00:11:49.050 --> 00:11:53.660
with other countries, but trying to make sure-there
is a new executive order on trying to study

00:11:53.660 --> 00:11:57.990
competition, make sure that agencies are thinking
about competitive landscapes of industries

00:11:57.990 --> 00:12:02.320
so that there's more competition in there,
more entry, so that new firms are getting

00:12:02.320 --> 00:12:06.769
into industries, because they're the firms
often that are innovating and, again, kind

00:12:06.769 --> 00:12:08.809
of pushing forward productivity.

00:12:08.809 --> 00:12:12.770
But lastly-and I think this is the one that
probably plays more to the business tax reform-is

00:12:12.770 --> 00:12:15.279
the question on investment.

00:12:15.279 --> 00:12:18.630
So one of the reasons productivity growth
has been slow in the U.S. is because capital

00:12:18.630 --> 00:12:23.399
deepening has slowed down, and a lot of that
is a hangover from the crisis still.

00:12:23.399 --> 00:12:25.649
That's starting to fade away.

00:12:25.649 --> 00:12:27.309
But some of that also goes back to demand.

00:12:27.309 --> 00:12:32.241
And this is where it's both demand in the
U.S. needs to be growing fast enough, but

00:12:32.241 --> 00:12:36.149
you know, when I think about competitiveness,
one of the things that always gets me is,

00:12:36.149 --> 00:12:37.899
you know, I want Canada to grow faster.

00:12:37.899 --> 00:12:41.819
I want nothing more than faster growth in
Europe and faster growth in Japan because

00:12:41.819 --> 00:12:46.839
I think it's-slow growth in Europe and Japan
has actually been part of what has slowed

00:12:46.839 --> 00:12:51.420
down investment in the United States, because
firms look not just at their own domestic

00:12:51.420 --> 00:12:52.509
base, but the world.

00:12:52.509 --> 00:12:55.629
And so I think all of those things would be
ways we could push.

00:12:55.629 --> 00:12:59.829
But I-just the last thing to say, as sort
of Steve said, I think the notion that we

00:12:59.829 --> 00:13:03.770
do not do enough to catch the people that
are getting hit by shocks, whether they're

00:13:03.770 --> 00:13:10.250
trade shocks or technology shocks or just
bad-luck shocks, I think is definitely true.

00:13:10.250 --> 00:13:14.850
And losing people out of the labor force is
one of the worst things we can do for them

00:13:14.850 --> 00:13:17.120
as individuals, but for the economy, too.

00:13:17.120 --> 00:13:22.689
PATEL: So if we view trade through the lens
of enhancing the U.S. as a business location,

00:13:22.689 --> 00:13:24.499
does TPP hurt or help?

00:13:24.499 --> 00:13:27.879
Is it that-you had mentioned these innovative
companies, they have better frameworks for

00:13:27.879 --> 00:13:29.370
doing their business.

00:13:29.370 --> 00:13:33.589
But then you could look at the downsides of,
you know, Japanese companies being able to

00:13:33.589 --> 00:13:36.259
make far cheaper cars with Chinese auto parts.

00:13:36.259 --> 00:13:41.649
SHAMBAUGH: I mean, I can say I think TPP unambiguously
helps U.S. firms.

00:13:41.649 --> 00:13:48.350
I think it makes-it makes it easier for U.S.
firms to export to a big and growing market.

00:13:48.350 --> 00:13:52.430
I think one part of that that's important
is globalization is happening.

00:13:52.430 --> 00:13:53.430
That's a fact.

00:13:53.430 --> 00:13:56.459
The question is, under what rules does globalization
happen?

00:13:56.459 --> 00:14:00.860
And it is absolutely in our interest and U.S.
firms' interest to have globalization happen

00:14:00.860 --> 00:14:05.660
on the terms that we're writing, as opposed
to terms that we don't have a hand in writing.

00:14:05.660 --> 00:14:09.160
And so, in that sense, I think, beyond the
fact that there's lots of stuff with trade

00:14:09.160 --> 00:14:14.740
facilitation and clearing out customs lags
and things like that that just makes it easier

00:14:14.740 --> 00:14:16.080
for U.S. firms.

00:14:16.080 --> 00:14:21.509
U.S. agriculture benefits massively from TPP
because U.S. ag is highly productive, and

00:14:21.509 --> 00:14:23.550
opening up markets to it does really well.

00:14:23.550 --> 00:14:28.610
So I think-I think you absolutely see a huge
benefit from TPP.

00:14:28.610 --> 00:14:34.620
RATTNER: But isn't it-didn't I just read-I
think it was ITC came out with a study that

00:14:34.620 --> 00:14:40.149
said TPP-to the point about winners and losers,
that TPP would benefit the economy as a whole

00:14:40.149 --> 00:14:44.870
all the ways you talked about, but that manufacturing-there
would actually be fewer manufacturing jobs

00:14:44.870 --> 00:14:47.480
as a result of TPP than otherwise?

00:14:47.480 --> 00:14:50.670
SHAMBAUGH: So I think there are two things
that are important there.

00:14:50.670 --> 00:14:55.110
The first is, when they run models like this,
they assume full employment at the start and

00:14:55.110 --> 00:14:59.610
the finish, so if any sector grows a different
sector has to shrink, and it's almost just

00:14:59.610 --> 00:15:02.050
an automatic fact of the way you run the model.

00:15:02.050 --> 00:15:04.110
That's not particularly helpful.

00:15:04.110 --> 00:15:07.610
But I think, to that point in general, it
is important to note that it's a question

00:15:07.610 --> 00:15:09.380
of how fast is it growing.

00:15:09.380 --> 00:15:14.209
So they still have manufacturing growing and
being bigger in the future than today, you

00:15:14.209 --> 00:15:16.259
know, regardless of TPP.

00:15:16.259 --> 00:15:18.800
It's a question of a very small differential
in growth rates.

00:15:18.800 --> 00:15:23.279
But the last thing is they left out, frankly,
a lot of the big benefits to trade that you

00:15:23.279 --> 00:15:28.980
see in some of the other academic studies
on TPP, where better firms within a sector

00:15:28.980 --> 00:15:31.600
are going to do better because they can get
to new markets.

00:15:31.600 --> 00:15:35.139
And that's where you get more productivity
growth out of trade, is the best firms are

00:15:35.139 --> 00:15:36.370
the ones that start exporting.

00:15:36.370 --> 00:15:41.179
They hire more workers; you've got more workers
working at the places that have better managers,

00:15:41.179 --> 00:15:45.250
better technology, better capital; and that
winds up being good.

00:15:45.250 --> 00:15:50.709
So I think, on average, even the ITC study,
that had limitations, said TPP is good for

00:15:50.709 --> 00:15:51.709
the U.S.

00:15:51.709 --> 00:15:56.240
I think the place that I would agree is any
shock, whether it's trade or technology, is

00:15:56.240 --> 00:15:58.519
going to have some reallocation.

00:15:58.519 --> 00:16:03.350
And that reallocation needs to be managed
right, because if you don't manage it right

00:16:03.350 --> 00:16:08.249
you wind up both with political issues, and
you wind up just losing people and leaving

00:16:08.249 --> 00:16:09.249
them behind.

00:16:09.249 --> 00:16:15.629
ENGLER: I think if you're a high-tech economy
such as we have, you put a great value, and

00:16:15.629 --> 00:16:21.199
we should put a greater value-and this agreement
moves strongly in that direction-on protecting

00:16:21.199 --> 00:16:22.730
your intellectual property.

00:16:22.730 --> 00:16:26.029
If you're going to make the investment in
R&amp;D-and our companies, just the Roundtable

00:16:26.029 --> 00:16:29.860
member companies, represent about two-thirds
of all the private-sector R&amp;D that's done

00:16:29.860 --> 00:16:35.200
annually, so just the 200 companies have that
big a footprint.

00:16:35.200 --> 00:16:41.100
But to continue to do that-and that's sort
of the fight within the TPP over biologics

00:16:41.100 --> 00:16:48.471
and the length of the protection because of
the expense there and the investment, can

00:16:48.471 --> 00:16:53.079
you recover it if you can't protect your patent
rights beyond a certain period.

00:16:53.079 --> 00:17:00.270
Well, we have the same thing in all kinds
of aspects of manufacturing and all-and I

00:17:00.270 --> 00:17:07.040
think even in services, in software, and how
we-and so this agreement breaks a lot of new

00:17:07.040 --> 00:17:11.800
ground in these areas, and it's really important,
and I think that's been under-recognized.

00:17:11.800 --> 00:17:18.150
And it creates some mechanisms for resolving
disputes when there's an allegation that there

00:17:18.150 --> 00:17:19.450
has been a violation.

00:17:19.450 --> 00:17:23.170
So all of that is there and very helpful.

00:17:23.170 --> 00:17:28.530
The other aspect about manufacturing, Steve,
which has been sort of a bugaboo of mine since

00:17:28.530 --> 00:17:32.770
I was over at the National Association of
Manufacturers, I think we've got a-we've talked

00:17:32.770 --> 00:17:37.610
to the Census Bureau about this, and it's
a great one for Commerce maybe to take up,

00:17:37.610 --> 00:17:39.790
but I don't think we count very well anymore.

00:17:39.790 --> 00:17:45.250
I mean, the secretary of agriculture is here,
and agriculture is growing in the United States.

00:17:45.250 --> 00:17:47.400
It's an important part of our economy.

00:17:47.400 --> 00:17:52.400
There's a lot fewer people on the farm, but
there's a whole lot of people in the agricultural

00:17:52.400 --> 00:17:59.120
world that are not only doing the science
on the front end, but on the processing and

00:17:59.120 --> 00:18:02.690
the shipping and the marketing and all that
on the back end.

00:18:02.690 --> 00:18:04.480
Well, something similar has happened in manufacturing.

00:18:04.480 --> 00:18:09.330
In the old manufacturing plant that might
have been four stories high, some of those

00:18:09.330 --> 00:18:13.410
floors were filled up with people who were
doing engineering or they were doing marketing,

00:18:13.410 --> 00:18:14.980
they were doing sales.

00:18:14.980 --> 00:18:17.860
A lot of that's been sourced today differently.

00:18:17.860 --> 00:18:19.900
They were running the transportation.

00:18:19.900 --> 00:18:21.290
Almost nobody's doing their own transportation.

00:18:21.290 --> 00:18:23.600
FedEx will take care of all that for you.

00:18:23.600 --> 00:18:25.680
Well, that's counted as a service.

00:18:25.680 --> 00:18:27.850
We don't get to count that in the manufacturing
sector.

00:18:27.850 --> 00:18:34.000
And I would argue that there's a significant
part of what is out there today as the service

00:18:34.000 --> 00:18:40.071
sector that is absolutely dependent on and
integral to manufacturing, and in the old

00:18:40.071 --> 00:18:41.920
days would have been counted as manufacturing.

00:18:41.920 --> 00:18:47.440
So the-so the heavy job losses in manufacturing,
no question there's fewer people on factory

00:18:47.440 --> 00:18:50.580
floors, and robots are going to make that
the case.

00:18:50.580 --> 00:18:55.020
And to Steve's point, I mean, the one thing
that's striking about auto plants, when I

00:18:55.020 --> 00:19:01.150
was governor, we-Michigan was-the first blow
to Michigan's auto industry, when I was a

00:19:01.150 --> 00:19:04.810
young legislator, was when General Motors
said they were going to put the Saturn plant

00:19:04.810 --> 00:19:06.800
down in Tennessee.

00:19:06.800 --> 00:19:13.480
And then we went all of those years, until
the 1990s, when we finally got two new auto

00:19:13.480 --> 00:19:16.350
plants put in Lansing, Michigan.

00:19:16.350 --> 00:19:20.730
And they tore down the old Oldsmobile factory
that we used to build the Oldsmobile there.

00:19:20.730 --> 00:19:22.021
They discontinued the brand.

00:19:22.021 --> 00:19:26.130
They built two new plants, and one of them
was their very successful Cadillac.

00:19:26.130 --> 00:19:30.320
But you were struck when you walked into that
new plant, where are the people?

00:19:30.320 --> 00:19:32.460
There was nobody there.

00:19:32.460 --> 00:19:36.970
They were building a lot of cars, but the
automation that had been put in that plant,

00:19:36.970 --> 00:19:39.330
that was how they were competing those wages.

00:19:39.330 --> 00:19:42.970
And I remember also at the Manufacturers going
to Alabama and-you know, I think Michigan

00:19:42.970 --> 00:19:47.620
in the '70s and '80s would have said, well,
you can't build cars in Alabama.

00:19:47.620 --> 00:19:50.690
I go down there, there's 100,000-plus autoworkers
in Alabama.

00:19:50.690 --> 00:19:53.540
What do they know about building cars?

00:19:53.540 --> 00:19:55.370
But that's where the growth had been taking
place.

00:19:55.370 --> 00:20:01.620
And so there's been a lot of disruption, some
domestic and some global.

00:20:01.620 --> 00:20:06.300
The one thing that we can't escape in all
of this, if people are going to survive that

00:20:06.300 --> 00:20:08.980
disruption, then it's how do we get the skills?

00:20:08.980 --> 00:20:13.090
And I would argue I think-I think Steve's
right that more could be done for people who

00:20:13.090 --> 00:20:14.980
are dislocated for trade.

00:20:14.980 --> 00:20:20.100
But frankly, more needs to be done for anybody
who's dislocated from a job, period.

00:20:20.100 --> 00:20:24.891
It's not just trade, because technology has
disrupted an awful lot of jobs, and that was

00:20:24.891 --> 00:20:27.640
U.S. technology disrupting a U.S. job.

00:20:27.640 --> 00:20:34.500
I mean, and so we don't have the flexible
workforce that we once prided ourselves because

00:20:34.500 --> 00:20:36.590
we're not getting them retrained.

00:20:36.590 --> 00:20:42.600
And one of the challenges there is that we
used to be able to sop up a lot of fairly

00:20:42.600 --> 00:20:44.250
unskilled workers.

00:20:44.250 --> 00:20:46.450
Today's economy can't do that.

00:20:46.450 --> 00:20:51.850
And so, if you're 50 years old and suddenly
are out on the street with some skills that

00:20:51.850 --> 00:20:58.140
you learned over a 25-year period in a job,
that might be not at all translatable to coding

00:20:58.140 --> 00:20:59.780
something or doing something in technology.

00:20:59.780 --> 00:21:02.500
And so we've got to rethink that.

00:21:02.500 --> 00:21:07.610
And part of it, as you're fighting poverty,
is you got to turn the supply chain off.

00:21:07.610 --> 00:21:11.570
We got to do a lot better job with our K-12
system because we can't have an America where

00:21:11.570 --> 00:21:17.090
36 percent of the kids, after four years in
a government school, are able to read proficiently.

00:21:17.090 --> 00:21:18.090
That's what the number is today.

00:21:18.090 --> 00:21:21.690
That's what America's report card is: 36 percent.

00:21:21.690 --> 00:21:23.560
The other 64 (percent), guess what?

00:21:23.560 --> 00:21:27.000
They're not going to do well, or they're not
going to do as well as they're capable of

00:21:27.000 --> 00:21:28.000
doing.

00:21:28.000 --> 00:21:30.460
And when they hit upper grades, they're the
likeliest to drop out.

00:21:30.460 --> 00:21:35.290
If they manage to get through school and get
enrolled in college, they're not very likely

00:21:35.290 --> 00:21:36.290
to finish.

00:21:36.290 --> 00:21:39.550
RATTNER: Look, I don't-can I say-I don't-I
don't disagree with any of that.

00:21:39.550 --> 00:21:44.430
I would just-I would just say that those hundred
thousand or however many there were autoworkers

00:21:44.430 --> 00:21:48.680
who went from Michigan to Alabama are making
about half as much as they were making back

00:21:48.680 --> 00:21:49.680
in Michigan.

00:21:49.680 --> 00:21:53.650
Now, we could-we could argue that, because
of unions or whatever reason, they were overpaid

00:21:53.650 --> 00:21:55.950
in Michigan, and this is just the market working.

00:21:55.950 --> 00:21:59.870
But we do, again, have to recognize that this
has real effects on people.

00:21:59.870 --> 00:22:06.310
I'm not an expert on how job classifications
are counted and categorized, but what I do

00:22:06.310 --> 00:22:11.381
know is that-and this may be a little surprising,
because everybody reads stories about the

00:22:11.381 --> 00:22:15.280
manufacturing renaissance in America-that
growth in-employment in manufacturing since

00:22:15.280 --> 00:22:19.780
the end of the recession has actually been
slower than growth in overall jobs in America-i.e.,

00:22:19.780 --> 00:22:22.460
manufacturing is losing share of employment.

00:22:22.460 --> 00:22:23.820
Manufacturing is also losing share of GDP.

00:22:23.820 --> 00:22:27.461
And I'm not here to be the guy who says the
sky is falling, but I'm just trying to be

00:22:27.461 --> 00:22:33.021
realistic about what I think-what I think
is achievable going forward, and make sure

00:22:33.021 --> 00:22:37.890
we don't sort of go off and set priorities
that are a bad use of government resources

00:22:37.890 --> 00:22:42.100
or private resources, or simply get people
to expect things that can't happen.

00:22:42.100 --> 00:22:46.170
I wanted to just add one thing to your original
list of things we should be doing, and I want

00:22:46.170 --> 00:22:49.450
to slightly disagree with you just to make
this an interesting conversation.

00:22:49.450 --> 00:22:50.450
ENGLER: Sure.

00:22:50.450 --> 00:22:52.420
RATTNER: And I think you've touched on much
of it, but let me put it together.

00:22:52.420 --> 00:22:55.930
I think the other place where a lot of work
can and should be done, that would really

00:22:55.930 --> 00:22:59.260
help our competitiveness, would be on our
federal spending priorities.

00:22:59.260 --> 00:23:03.230
We've basically been in a position where,
since all the austerity stuff started seven

00:23:03.230 --> 00:23:10.261
or so years ago, we've been-essentially had
very much across the board, I think I would

00:23:10.261 --> 00:23:13.440
argue, unthoughtful budget policy on the spending
side.

00:23:13.440 --> 00:23:19.100
And the result is that the spending reductions
or smaller increases that have occurred have

00:23:19.100 --> 00:23:23.500
come out of discretionary spending because
we haven't had really any consensus around

00:23:23.500 --> 00:23:24.780
addressing the entitlements program.

00:23:24.780 --> 00:23:30.010
And so things like R&amp;D and infrastructure
and education, things you mentioned-I don't

00:23:30.010 --> 00:23:35.373
think-you may not have mentioned infrastructure,
but I'm going to put that on the list if you

00:23:35.373 --> 00:23:36.373
didn't-
ENGLER: It should be on the list.

00:23:36.373 --> 00:23:37.373
I didn't get to it, but yeah.

00:23:37.373 --> 00:23:38.373
RATTNER: Yeah.

00:23:38.373 --> 00:23:39.373
So all those things are places where I think
the government has a role, and where I think

00:23:39.373 --> 00:23:43.530
the government for all the, again, obvious
political reasons we've been dealing with,

00:23:43.530 --> 00:23:47.050
I think has been-has been derelict in its
duty, and where I think it can play a much

00:23:47.050 --> 00:23:52.380
more positive force in enhancing our competitive
position.

00:23:52.380 --> 00:23:55.080
The one place I'm going to have to disagree
with you is on corporate tax reform.

00:23:55.080 --> 00:23:59.340
So, one, I agree completely we have to have
corporate tax reform.

00:23:59.340 --> 00:24:01.090
We are-we are not competitive.

00:24:01.090 --> 00:24:05.800
But the problem we have is that we're in a
kind of global race to the bottom.

00:24:05.800 --> 00:24:09.000
Every country is lowering its corporate tax
rate.

00:24:09.000 --> 00:24:12.540
Even the U.K., I think, is down to 21 or 20
percent, depending upon how you classify it.

00:24:12.540 --> 00:24:15.680
ENGLER: They'll go to 18 (percent) is what's
in their current law for the out years.

00:24:15.680 --> 00:24:16.680
RATTNER: OK.

00:24:16.680 --> 00:24:17.680
Thank you, right.

00:24:17.680 --> 00:24:22.890
And so I think we have to think about, how
do we-and so the-one of the things is that

00:24:22.890 --> 00:24:27.660
the share of corporate tax revenue as a percentage
of total federal revenue has been going down,

00:24:27.660 --> 00:24:29.060
down, down, down, down.

00:24:29.060 --> 00:24:33.600
And so I think it gets back to a little bit
of a fairness question of how do we ensure

00:24:33.600 --> 00:24:36.560
that all the participants in our society pay
their fair share.

00:24:36.560 --> 00:24:39.160
I mean, we can cut our tax rate to 18 percent.

00:24:39.160 --> 00:24:42.700
We can cut it to 12 1/2 percent, which is
I think Ireland's tax rate.

00:24:42.700 --> 00:24:43.700
I think Canada's 4 percent.

00:24:43.700 --> 00:24:44.780
We can do all that.

00:24:44.780 --> 00:24:49.560
But at some point we're then putting the burden
on other people, and we have to find some

00:24:49.560 --> 00:24:51.250
middle ground that's fairness.

00:24:51.250 --> 00:24:54.550
And then the very small point that I would
disagree with you about is the $2 trillion.

00:24:54.550 --> 00:24:58.680
It's not ideal that the $2 trillion is sitting
overseas, but I honestly don't believe-for

00:24:58.680 --> 00:25:03.720
reasons we can talk about and debate-that
it's having a material effect on investment

00:25:03.720 --> 00:25:07.490
in this country, because what happens is Apple
has all this cash trapped somewhere else,

00:25:07.490 --> 00:25:11.120
and so they go out and they borrow $5 billion
in the U.S. to either give it back to their

00:25:11.120 --> 00:25:13.140
shareholders or build plants or whatever it
is they're going to do with it.

00:25:13.140 --> 00:25:14.540
Money is fungible.

00:25:14.540 --> 00:25:18.920
And while it's a-while there's some inefficiencies
associated with the $2 trillion, I don't think

00:25:18.920 --> 00:25:23.710
it's on the top 10 list of things I worry
about in terms of what's holding back our

00:25:23.710 --> 00:25:24.710
competitive position.

00:25:24.710 --> 00:25:28.760
ENGLER: Well, I would argue that, that being
the case, then, why shouldn't we let them

00:25:28.760 --> 00:25:32.990
hold the debt and we'll keep the equity here,
rather than the reverse, which is what we've

00:25:32.990 --> 00:25:33.990
got today?

00:25:33.990 --> 00:25:36.600
The equity's out there and we get to borrow.

00:25:36.600 --> 00:25:38.010
I do think-
RATTNER: What do you mean, equity's out there?

00:25:38.010 --> 00:25:41.840
ENGLER: Well, in other words, the 2 trillion
(dollars), the real cash is there, and then

00:25:41.840 --> 00:25:42.840
Apple can borrow.

00:25:42.840 --> 00:25:43.840
RATTNER: Yeah, but that's-
ENGLER: I get it.

00:25:43.840 --> 00:25:45.960
RATTNER: But that's just sitting in a JPMorgan
bank account in London.

00:25:45.960 --> 00:25:46.960
What's the difference?

00:25:46.960 --> 00:25:51.660
ENGLER: Here's the difference, is that at
some point, if I bring that home and I've

00:25:51.660 --> 00:25:55.880
got to plus that up-and let's just use a 20
percent rate in the country where I earned

00:25:55.880 --> 00:25:59.500
it-I've got to pay 15 percent more, I'm going
to do what Microsoft did.

00:25:59.500 --> 00:26:03.830
I'm going to-because if I compound that with
the immigration problem, I can't get my talent

00:26:03.830 --> 00:26:06.800
here, can't get the money back, what are you
telling me?

00:26:06.800 --> 00:26:12.130
Go to Vancouver, build your R&amp;D center there
with that cash so you don't have to pay the

00:26:12.130 --> 00:26:16.950
extra 15 percent, and use the open immigration
law to get the best talent in the world there.

00:26:16.950 --> 00:26:22.580
That's our policy today, and that's the rational
decision based on the laws of the United States.

00:26:22.580 --> 00:26:24.410
That's what we're telling people.

00:26:24.410 --> 00:26:27.460
That's not what I think we should be saying,
but that is, in fact, our policy.

00:26:27.460 --> 00:26:28.862
RATTNER: Yeah, I would go through it a little
differently.

00:26:28.862 --> 00:26:31.660
I would say the reason they're building a
plant in Vancouver is because Canada as a

00:26:31.660 --> 00:26:34.310
4 percent tax rate and we have a 35 percent
tax rate.

00:26:34.310 --> 00:26:36.130
Again, I think the money can be moved around.

00:26:36.130 --> 00:26:41.190
I think the problem is our corporate tax rate
has made it unattractive for people to build

00:26:41.190 --> 00:26:42.190
plants here.

00:26:42.190 --> 00:26:43.190
I think that you and I would agree on that.

00:26:43.190 --> 00:26:45.790
SHAMBAUGH: And I think there is-there is a
way, though, to also try to do this that doesn't

00:26:45.790 --> 00:26:47.840
just encourage a race to the bottom purely,
right?

00:26:47.840 --> 00:26:53.360
And I think there-so I think the kind of broad
outlines the president put forward on business

00:26:53.360 --> 00:26:58.540
tax reform, one of them is you set some sort
of minimum tax rate firms are paying abroad,

00:26:58.540 --> 00:27:02.590
and as long as they're paying more than that
you're not paying the gap between 20 and 35

00:27:02.590 --> 00:27:03.590
(percent).

00:27:03.590 --> 00:27:05.740
Well, first of all, you'd lower the 35 (percent)
because you'd broaden the base.

00:27:05.740 --> 00:27:10.510
But also you would say locating somewhere
with a 4 percent tax rate, we are going to

00:27:10.510 --> 00:27:15.010
collect the gap to that, but if you locate
in a normal tax country then, as long as you

00:27:15.010 --> 00:27:17.120
pay your tax there, we're not going to hit
you here.

00:27:17.120 --> 00:27:22.120
And that would-could, in some sense, be the
first move of many countries trying to set

00:27:22.120 --> 00:27:26.310
a standard, where you say everyone's going
to tax you on if you locate in a tax haven,

00:27:26.310 --> 00:27:30.700
but we're not going to tax you on income in
a normal country, and that can try to break

00:27:30.700 --> 00:27:31.700
the cycle.

00:27:31.700 --> 00:27:35.580
Because I agree, you don't want to see corporate
taxes just fall to zero as a share of the

00:27:35.580 --> 00:27:39.000
economy; you just want to make sure that it's
not an incentive not to locate production

00:27:39.000 --> 00:27:40.000
in the United States.

00:27:40.000 --> 00:27:42.640
ENGLER: Well, actually, as the guy on the
panel here who's going to represent the working

00:27:42.640 --> 00:27:44.360
people, I would like to see it go to zero.

00:27:44.360 --> 00:27:48.690
I'd get rid of the corporate tax because,
as the economic studies show, that's a tax

00:27:48.690 --> 00:27:49.690
on labor.

00:27:49.690 --> 00:27:51.380
Where does the corporate burden go?

00:27:51.380 --> 00:27:53.840
It goes on-it goes on the worker.

00:27:53.840 --> 00:27:55.640
They bear the biggest brunt of the corporate
tax.

00:27:55.640 --> 00:27:59.790
So it's just a question-it's just another
way to collect taxes, but a(n) inefficient

00:27:59.790 --> 00:28:01.920
way which actually penalizes labor.

00:28:01.920 --> 00:28:06.210
Now, interestingly, what the Europeans are
up to, they say, well, look, U.S., if you're

00:28:06.210 --> 00:28:10.610
not going to bring the money back home, let's
us look at this on a country-by-country basis

00:28:10.610 --> 00:28:15.330
and see if we can't have a way to take a few
of those dollars off the table; we'll keep

00:28:15.330 --> 00:28:16.330
them in our treasuries.

00:28:16.330 --> 00:28:21.640
And that's our-that's our current fight right
now, is that, you know, so we're at some risk

00:28:21.640 --> 00:28:23.050
of that.

00:28:23.050 --> 00:28:26.380
I would-I just think the tax thing is a fairly
easy one.

00:28:26.380 --> 00:28:30.320
I want to-I want to spend a moment on the
point that Steve raised, because this is where

00:28:30.320 --> 00:28:31.940
we're in absolute violent agreement.

00:28:31.940 --> 00:28:36.220
And interestingly enough, it's the one area
where the two presidential-the two leading

00:28:36.220 --> 00:28:42.980
candidates, at least, you know, seem to be
at least willing to talk about, and that's

00:28:42.980 --> 00:28:43.980
infrastructure.

00:28:43.980 --> 00:28:47.510
And that's one area where we're dramatically
underinvested as a nation.

00:28:47.510 --> 00:28:55.081
And, in fact, there's a fairly large amount
of that that has just a flat-out ROI on the

00:28:55.081 --> 00:29:00.070
existing proposition without additional-it
doesn't need federal subsides.

00:29:00.070 --> 00:29:04.730
Now, you often need a federal permit, and
that sometimes is harder to get-easier to

00:29:04.730 --> 00:29:07.500
get a subsidy than a permit in some cases.

00:29:07.500 --> 00:29:13.210
But it's why you see the building trades going
berserk over the UAW and the AFL-CIO kind

00:29:13.210 --> 00:29:17.840
of getting in bed with Tom Steyer, because
they understand that on the radical environmental

00:29:17.840 --> 00:29:20.540
left they don't want to build anything.

00:29:20.540 --> 00:29:21.540
They don't want to put a shovel in the ground.

00:29:21.540 --> 00:29:23.440
They don't want to-they don't want to disturb
any soil.

00:29:23.440 --> 00:29:27.310
But if you're going to build a new bridge
where there was an old bridge, why do we spend

00:29:27.310 --> 00:29:29.370
five years on an environmental permit?

00:29:29.370 --> 00:29:31.570
Build the bridge.

00:29:31.570 --> 00:29:33.980
But we've got work to do on our ports.

00:29:33.980 --> 00:29:38.810
We've got work to do on everything from, you
know, certainly roads and bridges, which I

00:29:38.810 --> 00:29:40.730
think are the-actually the hardest one.

00:29:40.730 --> 00:29:45.370
Right now, we have in the Congress a bill
out of the House that would create a federally

00:29:45.370 --> 00:29:48.340
chartered corporation to run the air traffic
control system.

00:29:48.340 --> 00:29:51.990
This is something that started actually-Vice
President Gore was involved in this in the

00:29:51.990 --> 00:29:53.220
Clinton administration.

00:29:53.220 --> 00:29:56.760
It's had bipartisan support and secretary
of the treasury, supported by the air traffic

00:29:56.760 --> 00:30:01.600
controllers, the largest pilot union, and
we're not getting it done.

00:30:01.600 --> 00:30:06.250
And Steve's point is an interesting one because
we-why isn't it getting done?

00:30:06.250 --> 00:30:10.600
Well, we've got some people on the right who
think, gee, could there be federal spending

00:30:10.600 --> 00:30:11.600
involved?

00:30:11.600 --> 00:30:12.600
(Laughter.)

00:30:12.600 --> 00:30:13.680
Well, I don't know.

00:30:13.680 --> 00:30:17.430
We don't-we think this a, you know, standup
deal that, you know, a private-a federally

00:30:17.430 --> 00:30:20.880
chartered corporation is-it works.

00:30:20.880 --> 00:30:25.630
We've got appropriators who say, I'm going
to lose power if I can't appropriate the money,

00:30:25.630 --> 00:30:30.010
but you saw what happened when we had sequestration:
they folded like a cheap suit on the air traffic

00:30:30.010 --> 00:30:33.030
control system when they closed a couple of
towers.

00:30:33.030 --> 00:30:37.910
And then you've got some on the-kind of on
the left saying, well, because you have a

00:30:37.910 --> 00:30:43.050
small AFL-CIO union that's opposed, because
their jobs might be disrupted by the new system,

00:30:43.050 --> 00:30:48.870
because we use the best World War II technology
that we have available to us today.

00:30:48.870 --> 00:30:52.650
And we actually could upgrade that the way
others in the country-in the world have done.

00:30:52.650 --> 00:30:57.830
So we're getting-and we had-we had one member
who said, well, I'm not-I'm not for it because

00:30:57.830 --> 00:30:58.830
it might work.

00:30:58.830 --> 00:31:00.770
It's really hard to argue against that logic.

00:31:00.770 --> 00:31:01.770
(Laughter.)

00:31:01.770 --> 00:31:03.630
OK, yeah, we think it's going to work.

00:31:03.630 --> 00:31:08.710
But anyway, and the administration's neutral
because there is a-there is actually a split,

00:31:08.710 --> 00:31:11.750
although we think we've got more labor support
for it than not.

00:31:11.750 --> 00:31:15.170
But that would probably be a $30 billion project.

00:31:15.170 --> 00:31:19.210
We get a brand new air traffic control system
and complete it in three years.

00:31:19.210 --> 00:31:23.491
The FAA won't get it done by 2020, and the
National Academy says when they looked at

00:31:23.491 --> 00:31:26.470
it, if they build it the way they're building
it, when they get it done, it won't do what

00:31:26.470 --> 00:31:28.230
was promised to do in the first place.

00:31:28.230 --> 00:31:34.060
I mean, that's the kind of stuff-we have to
disrupt the status quo in the government to

00:31:34.060 --> 00:31:37.330
let us get at how we fix these things.

00:31:37.330 --> 00:31:39.410
The grid could be upgraded and hardened.

00:31:39.410 --> 00:31:41.760
That ought to be paid for by rate payers.

00:31:41.760 --> 00:31:45.380
But we can't figure out how to get across
five states with a power line.

00:31:45.380 --> 00:31:49.480
And Tom and I know that you tell the governors
you got six months to work it out or a federal

00:31:49.480 --> 00:31:55.320
decision will take care of it, virtually all
of us would-OK, we'll get it done and figured

00:31:55.320 --> 00:31:56.320
out.

00:31:56.320 --> 00:31:58.780
PATEL: Well, I always appreciate opening it
up to questions when the panel's in violent

00:31:58.780 --> 00:31:59.840
agreement about something.

00:31:59.840 --> 00:32:03.110
SHAMBAUGH: Just to echo the violent agreement:
strongly in favor of more infrastructure,

00:32:03.110 --> 00:32:04.260
just if that wasn't clear.

00:32:04.260 --> 00:32:05.260
PATEL: (Laughs.)

00:32:05.260 --> 00:32:06.260
Excellent.

00:32:06.260 --> 00:32:09.860
So, at this time, I'll invite members to join
the conversation with their questions.

00:32:09.860 --> 00:32:12.130
And as a reminder, this meeting is on the
record.

00:32:12.130 --> 00:32:15.110
Please wait for the microphone and speak directly
into it.

00:32:15.110 --> 00:32:20.280
Stand, state your name and affiliation, and
please limit yourself to one question.

00:32:20.280 --> 00:32:24.410
And it would be fantastic if you could keep
it concise in order to allow as many members

00:32:24.410 --> 00:32:28.280
as possible to speak.

00:32:28.280 --> 00:32:31.590
Q: Ted Alden from the Council on Foreign Relations.

00:32:31.590 --> 00:32:33.400
Thank you for a very rich discussion.

00:32:33.400 --> 00:32:37.340
And I was actually delighted to see you come
out on corporate tax and infrastructure, which

00:32:37.340 --> 00:32:41.180
in the book we just came out with and Mira
mentioned, those are the two areas that we

00:32:41.180 --> 00:32:44.800
argued ought to be the easiest to resolve,
but even those ones don't turn out to be all

00:32:44.800 --> 00:32:45.800
that easy to resolve.

00:32:45.800 --> 00:32:47.830
I mean, education is hard-fixing education
is hard-

00:32:47.830 --> 00:32:49.174
ENGLER: I think a few of them actually can
go together too, by the way, if you an have

00:32:49.174 --> 00:32:50.174
interest in them.

00:32:50.174 --> 00:32:51.174
Q: Yeah, no, and they could-they could quite
naturally.

00:32:51.174 --> 00:32:54.990
And my question actually is for Jay Shambaugh
and if anybody else wants to respond in.

00:32:54.990 --> 00:32:56.950
You said it's good for us if other countries
grow.

00:32:56.950 --> 00:32:57.950
No question about that.

00:32:57.950 --> 00:33:00.040
You know, if other countries are growing faster,
that's more opportunities.

00:33:00.040 --> 00:33:01.470
Do the sources of the growth matter?

00:33:01.470 --> 00:33:05.170
And this kind of gets into the currency and
export driven-you know, China-the percentage

00:33:05.170 --> 00:33:09.740
of Chinese growth that's coming out of domestic
consumption is very low compared to other

00:33:09.740 --> 00:33:10.740
countries.

00:33:10.740 --> 00:33:15.230
Does it matter to us whether China or Germany
are growing through exports or whether it's

00:33:15.230 --> 00:33:16.230
domestically driven?

00:33:16.230 --> 00:33:17.770
Should we care about the sources of those
growth?

00:33:17.770 --> 00:33:23.310
SHAMBAUGH: So, the short answer is usually
we don't care as much, but right now we care

00:33:23.310 --> 00:33:24.310
more.

00:33:24.310 --> 00:33:29.840
And the reason for that is in a-in a world
where you-where you see interest rates basically

00:33:29.840 --> 00:33:36.030
at zero around the world, and you see effectively
every major central bank under their inflation

00:33:36.030 --> 00:33:41.700
target and trying to lift demand and lift
growth, then there is this kind of-I don't

00:33:41.700 --> 00:33:47.290
want to say predatory-but there is this aspect
where demand is in finite supply, in a sense.

00:33:47.290 --> 00:33:52.020
And when Germany runs a(n) almost 9 percent
current account surplus, that means they're

00:33:52.020 --> 00:33:55.870
making a lot more stuff than they're consuming
and investing, and it would be better for

00:33:55.870 --> 00:33:59.880
the world-certainly better for Europe-if they
were to rebalance that to some extent.

00:33:59.880 --> 00:34:05.280
I don't think this is the most important issue
on Earth, but I think the places in the world

00:34:05.280 --> 00:34:09.760
that could lift global demand themselves,
because there are large countries that have

00:34:09.760 --> 00:34:11.750
room to do so, it would be helpful if they
did.

00:34:11.750 --> 00:34:12.940
And I think that's the idea.

00:34:12.940 --> 00:34:19.000
I think in normal times, you care a lot less
because you assume that central banks, and

00:34:19.000 --> 00:34:22.819
interest rates, and exchange rates adjust
across the world, and what you really care

00:34:22.819 --> 00:34:26.550
about is your own productivity growth, and
a lot of the other stuff will wash out.

00:34:26.550 --> 00:34:29.690
I think there is a slightly special case now,
is the issue.

00:34:29.690 --> 00:34:32.480
RATTNER: All right, we're done.

00:34:32.480 --> 00:34:33.589
SHAMBAUGH: Yeah.

00:34:33.589 --> 00:34:34.589
(Chuckles.)

00:34:34.589 --> 00:34:39.169
PATEL: Steve, I think you had something to
say just before we leaped into this question

00:34:39.169 --> 00:34:40.169
period.

00:34:40.169 --> 00:34:41.169
Love to return to that, if you want to share.

00:34:41.169 --> 00:34:43.589
RATTNER: I was going to-I was going to make-yeah,
I was going to go back to corporate taxes

00:34:43.589 --> 00:34:49.230
and say, very simply, that I'd be perfectly
happy to cut corporate taxes by any amount

00:34:49.230 --> 00:34:54.609
you want to as long as you offset it by eliminating
the preference for dividends and capital gains,

00:34:54.609 --> 00:34:59.240
because if you want to-if your-if your argument
is you want labor to be-why should labor and

00:34:59.240 --> 00:35:03.240
capital be taxes differently, that's a place
where we are at the moment: taxing labor and

00:35:03.240 --> 00:35:04.730
capital quite differently.

00:35:04.730 --> 00:35:10.150
ENGLER: Well, we'll have to have a long conversation
about that, because there-but there are some

00:35:10.150 --> 00:35:14.380
opportunities to have a much more competitive
tax code-a simpler tax code.

00:35:14.380 --> 00:35:19.509
And what we've got today is a mess and it's
way out of date.

00:35:19.509 --> 00:35:20.509
RATTNER: Well, that's all true.

00:35:20.509 --> 00:35:25.210
I mean, as I've said, we've not had comprehensive
tax reform in 30 years and it's like a garden:

00:35:25.210 --> 00:35:27.890
you have to keep tending it, and watering
it, and weeding it.

00:35:27.890 --> 00:35:31.170
Otherwise, people find ways around it and
we're certainly at that place.

00:35:31.170 --> 00:35:32.170
PATEL: Right.

00:35:32.170 --> 00:35:34.490
And great, a question in the middle.

00:35:34.490 --> 00:35:36.369
Q: Thank you.

00:35:36.369 --> 00:35:38.249
Mohammed Khaishgi from The Resource Group.

00:35:38.249 --> 00:35:43.661
I had a question as, I guess, in the context
of the tech sector, where, I guess, there's

00:35:43.661 --> 00:35:49.740
consensus that the innovation and the creativity
that the U.S. represents with all of the-with

00:35:49.740 --> 00:35:55.501
all of the, I guess, assets it possesses is-cannot
be rivaled anywhere else in the world, or

00:35:55.501 --> 00:35:56.940
at least most places in the world.

00:35:56.940 --> 00:36:00.980
To what extent-on the presumption that the
tech sector provides some sort of a leading

00:36:00.980 --> 00:36:05.509
indication of what the mainstream economy
is going to look like in the future-to what

00:36:05.509 --> 00:36:11.009
extent do you think that portends kind of-maybe
positive thoughts about the competitiveness

00:36:11.009 --> 00:36:13.299
of the U.S. sort of going forward?

00:36:13.299 --> 00:36:14.650
Thank you.

00:36:14.650 --> 00:36:19.450
SHAMBAUGH: One thing I would say just quickly
is, I mean, I think it is a positive sign.

00:36:19.450 --> 00:36:22.570
Like, if you sit there and say, what are the
key components of productivity, innovation

00:36:22.570 --> 00:36:23.570
is one of them.

00:36:23.570 --> 00:36:27.770
And I think as we were talking beforehand,
the U.S. tends to score very well on measures

00:36:27.770 --> 00:36:29.420
around that.

00:36:29.420 --> 00:36:33.970
I think we've been studying a lot concerns
about slower productivity growth at the CEA,

00:36:33.970 --> 00:36:37.759
and we always joke that, now if you were to
go around Silicon Valley and tell people productivity

00:36:37.759 --> 00:36:41.859
growth has been low, they would look at you
as if you're insane and say, how is that possible?

00:36:41.859 --> 00:36:44.740
We're creating so many new things constantly.

00:36:44.740 --> 00:36:49.400
And so, sometimes it takes a while for innovations
in one space to spill more broadly across

00:36:49.400 --> 00:36:50.400
the economy.

00:36:50.400 --> 00:36:54.299
There was the famous quip in the late '80s:
I see computers everywhere, but the productivity

00:36:54.299 --> 00:36:55.400
statistics.

00:36:55.400 --> 00:36:57.730
And then, boy, they hit the productivity statistics.

00:36:57.730 --> 00:37:02.930
And so, at-you know, that's the optimistic
case is that some combination of hangover

00:37:02.930 --> 00:37:06.619
from the financial crisis and a number of
other things has kept some of the great innovations

00:37:06.619 --> 00:37:08.509
from spilling more broadly.

00:37:08.509 --> 00:37:13.390
But I think it's always dangerous to buy the
optimistic case too wholeheartedly, and to

00:37:13.390 --> 00:37:15.849
always keep an eye on some of the other things
you worry about.

00:37:15.849 --> 00:37:17.759
ENGLER: Go ahead, Steve.

00:37:17.759 --> 00:37:22.390
RATTNER: Well, I spend a fair amount of time
out in the bay area and there's no question

00:37:22.390 --> 00:37:26.480
that the tech sector is one of our enormous
competitive strengths.

00:37:26.480 --> 00:37:32.980
And if you-if you look-if you look at all
the core technology services that we all use

00:37:32.980 --> 00:37:39.630
and people around the world all use, the vast,
vast, with a few-only a few exceptions, are

00:37:39.630 --> 00:37:40.920
American companies.

00:37:40.920 --> 00:37:43.029
And I think that can only be good for us.

00:37:43.029 --> 00:37:46.609
But it leads me to a related point, which
is-I also sort of learned in my auto experience,

00:37:46.609 --> 00:37:49.820
and I say to people-when people say, gee,
it's really great, you saved General Motors

00:37:49.820 --> 00:37:50.820
and all the stuff.

00:37:50.820 --> 00:37:55.099
And I say, well, we saved the companies, but
I'm not as sure whether we saved the jobs.

00:37:55.099 --> 00:37:56.490
And they're really two different things.

00:37:56.490 --> 00:38:01.309
And, you know, it's great that General Motors
is very successful, and makes cars all over

00:38:01.309 --> 00:38:03.150
the world, and is competitive.

00:38:03.150 --> 00:38:08.849
But I think what ultimately-what we ultimately
care the most about are jobs here, and what

00:38:08.849 --> 00:38:11.710
ultimately drives our GDP are jobs here.

00:38:11.710 --> 00:38:16.790
And so, it's not enough to have a great American
company that's competitive around the world

00:38:16.790 --> 00:38:20.069
against other foreign-against foreign companies.

00:38:20.069 --> 00:38:24.609
But if they're not making and doing it here,
then we really haven't gained all that much

00:38:24.609 --> 00:38:26.670
in terms of what I think we're really trying
to accomplish.

00:38:26.670 --> 00:38:31.690
ENGLER: And I think today, the technology
of the car, I know it's more expensive and

00:38:31.690 --> 00:38:37.509
greater cost for the technology than it is
for the steel or the aluminum that's in a

00:38:37.509 --> 00:38:38.509
car.

00:38:38.509 --> 00:38:42.250
One of the other areas where I think there's
been a, you know, a bit of a disconnect, especially

00:38:42.250 --> 00:38:52.299
in recent years, we've just had three years
where small business formation has fallen

00:38:52.299 --> 00:38:54.910
below small business closure.

00:38:54.910 --> 00:38:58.700
And small business has always been one of
those little engines that would take that

00:38:58.700 --> 00:39:03.210
disruptive technology or that new idea and
try to go blow something up.

00:39:03.210 --> 00:39:04.210
(Laughter.)

00:39:04.210 --> 00:39:07.900
And if we're-and we have-and what-something
that hasn't been said here today, but there

00:39:07.900 --> 00:39:14.549
is a massive regulatory overhang that has
been created, you know, where-three years,

00:39:14.549 --> 00:39:17.770
four, I think, now, 80,000 plus pages of new
regulations.

00:39:17.770 --> 00:39:23.900
So, I mean, we're getting-we're getting more
government than we really want here.

00:39:23.900 --> 00:39:30.240
And in some cases, you know, some of the companies
that maybe belong to the Roundtable, they

00:39:30.240 --> 00:39:32.540
can bake that into their cost of business.

00:39:32.540 --> 00:39:39.589
It's expensive, but the small person says,
I'm not even going to try; I'm getting out

00:39:39.589 --> 00:39:40.589
of here.

00:39:40.589 --> 00:39:43.670
SHAMBAUGH: So, one thing-just to respond,
because I completely agree: the concern, you

00:39:43.670 --> 00:39:46.599
know, we often refer to as business dynamism
on the decline.

00:39:46.599 --> 00:39:51.339
I mean, one thing that is the case is when
you look at the research, it's been declining

00:39:51.339 --> 00:39:52.339
for 30 years.

00:39:52.339 --> 00:39:56.440
And that's one of these trends that is so
important to try to push back on because I

00:39:56.440 --> 00:40:01.329
agree entirely that if you don't have enough
startups, that does make it harder to get

00:40:01.329 --> 00:40:03.319
those innovations to pivot over.

00:40:03.319 --> 00:40:08.140
But I also think it's a-it's a longer trend
than anything recent that we have to be thinking

00:40:08.140 --> 00:40:09.400
about pushing back on.

00:40:09.400 --> 00:40:12.549
RATTNER: And I think it's part of a maturing-there's
so many hands, I don't want to say much-but

00:40:12.549 --> 00:40:14.010
I think it's part of a maturing economy.

00:40:14.010 --> 00:40:17.319
In other words, who's going to start a company
to compete against Walmart or something like

00:40:17.319 --> 00:40:18.319
that?

00:40:18.319 --> 00:40:19.319
You've got very established companies.

00:40:19.319 --> 00:40:23.239
I just know that when I go to the Valley and
to San Francisco, the dynamism, the entrepreneurship,

00:40:23.239 --> 00:40:25.420
the innovation is really extraordinary.

00:40:25.420 --> 00:40:27.920
PATEL: Great.

00:40:27.920 --> 00:40:31.870
So, we'll start in the back.

00:40:31.870 --> 00:40:32.870
Q: Thea Lee with the AFL-CIO.

00:40:32.870 --> 00:40:34.390
Thanks for an interesting discussion.

00:40:34.390 --> 00:40:39.759
And I think that the point that Ted raised
and that Steve mentioned a little while ago

00:40:39.759 --> 00:40:45.220
is important, that the-what we aren't really
dealing with here is job creation in the United

00:40:45.220 --> 00:40:46.269
States, income distribution.

00:40:46.269 --> 00:40:51.309
And, you know, Jay, you said earlier that
the key is productivity.

00:40:51.309 --> 00:40:55.940
But productivity's been rising for a couple
of decades and wages at the median level have

00:40:55.940 --> 00:40:58.220
not been keeping up-real wages.

00:40:58.220 --> 00:41:01.589
And so, that's really a question of bargaining
power and it's a question of policies.

00:41:01.589 --> 00:41:06.809
And I agree with all of you that, you know,
infrastructure is a key and investment in

00:41:06.809 --> 00:41:07.809
infrastructure.

00:41:07.809 --> 00:41:12.299
But I think that you do come into a dilemma
there with the tax issue.

00:41:12.299 --> 00:41:17.640
And if you say that the key thing, John, about,
you know, that we need to do for competitiveness

00:41:17.640 --> 00:41:22.569
is to get-take corporate taxes to zero, how
are we going to pay for the infrastructure

00:41:22.569 --> 00:41:23.569
that we need?

00:41:23.569 --> 00:41:26.089
Are we going to raise personal income taxes?

00:41:26.089 --> 00:41:27.990
Are we going to go to a value-added tax?

00:41:27.990 --> 00:41:32.040
You know, one of the things I think has been
frustrating for the labor movement when we

00:41:32.040 --> 00:41:36.000
talk about corporate tax reform is this idea
that you need revenue neutral or you need

00:41:36.000 --> 00:41:38.170
to cut taxes for corporations.

00:41:38.170 --> 00:41:41.849
And I agree with Steve: you need to figure
out how everybody's paying their fair share

00:41:41.849 --> 00:41:45.300
because we're all using the resources that
government provides.

00:41:45.300 --> 00:41:47.790
And neither education nor infrastructure is
cheap.

00:41:47.790 --> 00:41:52.280
So, I guess I want to hear, you know, in terms
of the tax reform, and competitiveness, and

00:41:52.280 --> 00:41:57.589
corporate taxes, how does that fit in with
the burning need that we have to invest in

00:41:57.589 --> 00:41:58.589
the future?

00:41:58.589 --> 00:42:04.049
And then I also think-I feel like you all
have not discussed enough the issues about

00:42:04.049 --> 00:42:09.960
unfair trade practices and how other countries'
actions-whether it's in currency manipulation

00:42:09.960 --> 00:42:14.880
or violation of labor standards and environmental
standards-how that impacts the United States

00:42:14.880 --> 00:42:20.039
as we try to figure out how our businesses
can succeed and create good jobs here in the

00:42:20.039 --> 00:42:21.039
United States.

00:42:21.039 --> 00:42:28.049
ENGLER: I think-I'll take a-take a shot at
part of that just on the broader tax reform

00:42:28.049 --> 00:42:30.450
question first.

00:42:30.450 --> 00:42:34.089
We've never taken the position that we need
to have some net reduction.

00:42:34.089 --> 00:42:38.559
What we needed and thought this was almost
kind of a, you know, fiscally sustainable

00:42:38.559 --> 00:42:44.170
approach to do this where we're, you know,
willing to give up-and think it actually makes

00:42:44.170 --> 00:42:48.540
smart tax policy to give up certain elements
of the tax code in order to get the rate down-get

00:42:48.540 --> 00:42:50.759
a flatter, broader base.

00:42:50.759 --> 00:42:52.650
And we-and we think that makes sense.

00:42:52.650 --> 00:42:56.000
Unfortunately, a lot of the other people who
want to go along for the ride have a little

00:42:56.000 --> 00:43:01.930
different view and don't have any way to pay
for the relief they'd like to have.

00:43:01.930 --> 00:43:05.980
But that's not true with the corporate taxpayers.

00:43:05.980 --> 00:43:14.099
I think the job creation here, when you talk
about funding infrastructure, I think there's

00:43:14.099 --> 00:43:19.079
a lot of infrastructure that, frankly, I mean-I
mean, I look at the Mississippi River.

00:43:19.079 --> 00:43:22.569
Half the commerce in the country can move
on the Mississippi.

00:43:22.569 --> 00:43:28.839
There are already revenue streams that exist
for shipping, for using locks and dams, for

00:43:28.839 --> 00:43:32.240
recreation purposes.

00:43:32.240 --> 00:43:37.390
We've got a(n) almost unlimited capacity right
now, I think, as a country to absorb 40-,

00:43:37.390 --> 00:43:39.589
50-year bonds on these kinds of projects.

00:43:39.589 --> 00:43:47.260
What we can't do in this case get by the Army
Corps and the laborious process to get things

00:43:47.260 --> 00:43:48.260
done.

00:43:48.260 --> 00:43:55.290
Again, Governor Vilsack knows full well, but,
I mean, there are recreation, agricultural,

00:43:55.290 --> 00:43:57.119
industrial, flood control.

00:43:57.119 --> 00:44:00.410
I mean, there's all kinds of issues there.

00:44:00.410 --> 00:44:07.619
But that is a 30-year project that-it's many,
many billions of dollars, and-but we don't

00:44:07.619 --> 00:44:16.289
have the way to manage these under the current
dispersion of authority across the governments

00:44:16.289 --> 00:44:19.589
at the federal level and up and down.

00:44:19.589 --> 00:44:23.200
And I think you're going to-it's going to
take new mechanisms, and we're all going to

00:44:23.200 --> 00:44:28.099
have to stand up and say, you know, if we're
going to dredge a harbor somewhere, it was

00:44:28.099 --> 00:44:33.319
a harbor before, it's still going to be a
harbor, but by dredging it, we make it, you

00:44:33.319 --> 00:44:36.020
know, much more commercially successful.

00:44:36.020 --> 00:44:43.269
So, I just think we have not committed ourselves
and had the national leadership.

00:44:43.269 --> 00:44:48.660
This goes back-this is not a-this is going
back quite some time now.

00:44:48.660 --> 00:44:53.279
The governors are trying, but we don't have
the revenue sources at the state level alone.

00:44:53.279 --> 00:44:55.589
You need to get access to the federal tools.

00:44:55.589 --> 00:44:58.930
PATEL: In the front.

00:44:58.930 --> 00:45:01.430
Q: Thank you.

00:45:01.430 --> 00:45:03.940
I'm Paula Stern.

00:45:03.940 --> 00:45:13.950
I feel like this conversation about competitiveness
is kind of looking backwards even though we

00:45:13.950 --> 00:45:15.609
should be looking forward.

00:45:15.609 --> 00:45:26.029
And I'd like to ask you to think about productivity
in the context of the gig economy, in the

00:45:26.029 --> 00:45:32.420
context of the fact that we're in a digital
age, we're leaving the manufacturing age,

00:45:32.420 --> 00:45:43.450
and what it is that a national leadership
should be doing to reshape both our retraining

00:45:43.450 --> 00:45:56.299
programs as well as deal with the safety nets
that are actually required to assure that

00:45:56.299 --> 00:45:59.839
you get the productivity out of a gig economy.

00:45:59.839 --> 00:46:01.589
Could you address that?

00:46:01.589 --> 00:46:07.569
I appreciate-the auto industry is very important
and manufacturing's very important, but the

00:46:07.569 --> 00:46:11.130
numbers are shrinking in the manufacturing
sector.

00:46:11.130 --> 00:46:18.640
And I'd really love to hear the benefit of
all three of y'all's very thoughtful experience

00:46:18.640 --> 00:46:20.869
on what we should be doing going forward.

00:46:20.869 --> 00:46:21.869
OK?

00:46:21.869 --> 00:46:25.900
SHAMBAUGH: So, I mean-one thing I would just
say really quickly, and it comes back to the

00:46:25.900 --> 00:46:31.710
previous question as well, is that productivity
is a necessary but not sufficient condition

00:46:31.710 --> 00:46:34.599
for broadly shared rising living standards,
right?

00:46:34.599 --> 00:46:40.380
So, you need more output per hour worked to
make living standards go up, but to make sure

00:46:40.380 --> 00:46:46.369
they go up for everybody, you need to both
make sure that that's showing up in wages

00:46:46.369 --> 00:46:51.730
on the one hand, and you need to make sure
that any kind of increase in productivity-whether

00:46:51.730 --> 00:46:55.930
it's technological, trade, whatever it is-is
going to have people following who are going

00:46:55.930 --> 00:46:56.930
to be shifting.

00:46:56.930 --> 00:46:59.890
They're going to be reallocating, they're
going to be dislocated.

00:46:59.890 --> 00:47:03.130
And I think most of the research over the
last 20 years has suggested that's a place

00:47:03.130 --> 00:47:05.309
where we don't do as well as many other countries.

00:47:05.309 --> 00:47:07.109
We don't do as well on retraining.

00:47:07.109 --> 00:47:10.999
We don't do as well at making sure people
don't fall out of the labor force.

00:47:10.999 --> 00:47:13.299
And I think those aspects are really important.

00:47:13.299 --> 00:47:19.089
On the specifics of it being a digital economy
or a gig economy, I think those do, you know,

00:47:19.089 --> 00:47:23.309
I think-I think those raise particular challenges
in terms of how do you deal with them in terms

00:47:23.309 --> 00:47:26.480
of older style programs that were supposed
to catch people.

00:47:26.480 --> 00:47:31.839
I think certainly things like, I mean, we
put in the 2017 budget wage insurance that,

00:47:31.839 --> 00:47:35.119
you know, is the kind of thing where to try
to make sure people who are dislocated find

00:47:35.119 --> 00:47:39.440
themselves back into a job, and even if it's
at a lower wage for a while, the government's

00:47:39.440 --> 00:47:41.140
going to help, things like that.

00:47:41.140 --> 00:47:45.200
The gig economy itself, because it's all self-employed,
some of the programs, you know, what does

00:47:45.200 --> 00:47:49.860
it mean to be unemployed if you're self-employed,
things like that get-that I think get a lot

00:47:49.860 --> 00:47:53.960
more challenging and probably longer than
I can talk about right now, but I'll-

00:47:53.960 --> 00:47:57.420
RATTNER: I'll just add a couple points to
the-first, what always strikes me when I do

00:47:57.420 --> 00:48:03.420
go out to the Valley area is that while I
am-I'll grossly exaggerate or generalize a

00:48:03.420 --> 00:48:07.499
bit, while I suspect that a huge percentage
of the people who, you know, who are all very

00:48:07.499 --> 00:48:11.420
young, of course, who I'm interacting with
are probably voting more on the Democratic

00:48:11.420 --> 00:48:14.059
side, they're also almost libertarians.

00:48:14.059 --> 00:48:18.109
Their kind of view of life is if government
would just get out of our way, you know, we

00:48:18.109 --> 00:48:20.869
would innovate and we would create this great
world.

00:48:20.869 --> 00:48:22.999
So, they have-they have a somewhat different
perspective.

00:48:22.999 --> 00:48:25.309
So, I'm not going to repeat everything that
Jay said, which I agree with.

00:48:25.309 --> 00:48:28.059
I would just add a couple-a couple of thoughts.

00:48:28.059 --> 00:48:31.630
One is, I think, you know-I'm not sure what,
you know-people talk about the gig economy

00:48:31.630 --> 00:48:35.170
meaning more the sharing economy, what-you
know, being an Uber driver or this that, or

00:48:35.170 --> 00:48:36.359
they also mean the tech world.

00:48:36.359 --> 00:48:38.890
I'm not sure which one you're-Paula, you're
talking about.

00:48:38.890 --> 00:48:43.930
But let me just-but I think when we talk about-
Q: I'm talking about the self-employed, the-

00:48:43.930 --> 00:48:44.930
RATTNER: OK.

00:48:44.930 --> 00:48:46.021
Well, let me address that because that's where
I was going.

00:48:46.021 --> 00:48:49.049
I think when you talk about a world in which
more people are self-employed, or are contract

00:48:49.049 --> 00:48:53.440
employees, or working part-time, which I think
is, on balance, a real plus for the economy.

00:48:53.440 --> 00:48:54.440
It's more flexibility.

00:48:54.440 --> 00:48:57.940
I don't know how many of you ask an Uber driver
when you get in, one, whether they like their

00:48:57.940 --> 00:48:58.940
job or not.

00:48:58.940 --> 00:49:02.279
But I do, and I think the vast preponderance
would love the opportunity to be able to do

00:49:02.279 --> 00:49:03.279
that.

00:49:03.279 --> 00:49:08.059
But I do think it probably necessitates some
updating of our-some of our labor rules around

00:49:08.059 --> 00:49:11.130
things like overtime and how contract employees
are treated.

00:49:11.130 --> 00:49:15.089
I think it requires some updating or some
rethinking of how benefits are conferred and

00:49:15.089 --> 00:49:18.657
making benefits more portable so that people
can do a little of this and a little of that

00:49:18.657 --> 00:49:22.489
and still have healthcare, or still have a
pension, or still have whatever it is they

00:49:22.489 --> 00:49:23.650
need for security.

00:49:23.650 --> 00:49:27.299
And again, I just-I think this is a place
where there are a lot of good ideas around,

00:49:27.299 --> 00:49:30.329
but we just haven't done very much.

00:49:30.329 --> 00:49:37.809
ENGLER: I'll put one piece in there that I
think is essential, and that's we spend $650

00:49:37.809 --> 00:49:41.760
billion as a year on-as a nation every year
on K-12 education.

00:49:41.760 --> 00:49:46.230
We are not getting our money's worth.

00:49:46.230 --> 00:49:52.500
And we cannot have the lack of performance
that we have there and hope to succeed in

00:49:52.500 --> 00:49:54.479
any of the things we're talking about here.

00:49:54.479 --> 00:49:58.390
Because even if we get into infrastructure,
which-one of the things that we didn't mention,

00:49:58.390 --> 00:50:04.029
but I would mention it, it is a tremendous
opportunity to do massive job training because

00:50:04.029 --> 00:50:07.549
we don't have the workers to do the infrastructure
that we'd like to build.

00:50:07.549 --> 00:50:09.650
They're just not there and ready to go.

00:50:09.650 --> 00:50:14.359
We have 5 million-some say 5.8 million-jobs
unfilled in the country today.

00:50:14.359 --> 00:50:19.460
And-you know, those are jobs that, for the
most part, we don't have the skills lined

00:50:19.460 --> 00:50:21.049
up with where the vacancies are.

00:50:21.049 --> 00:50:26.559
Now, oddly enough, as a country, we seem to
be less mobile today.

00:50:26.559 --> 00:50:30.839
Information moves everywhere, but people don't
seem to move very much like they once did.

00:50:30.839 --> 00:50:36.420
I heard one that got to ask this question
about-a member of Congress from South Dakota

00:50:36.420 --> 00:50:39.970
who said, look, we've got all these jobs;
why won't they move there?

00:50:39.970 --> 00:50:41.040
Well, weather, maybe?

00:50:41.040 --> 00:50:44.329
You know, I don't-you know, but that's just
they don't want to-anyway.

00:50:44.329 --> 00:50:48.999
Education, though, we-and I'll give you a
statistic: Detroit public schools, because

00:50:48.999 --> 00:50:53.369
they've been in the news lately, and I'm not
there, but I try to-I follow a little bit,

00:50:53.369 --> 00:50:58.470
and 5 percent of the children who take the
NAEP test, the national reading test on reading,

00:50:58.470 --> 00:51:01.970
5 percent can read proficiently after the
fourth grade.

00:51:01.970 --> 00:51:04.390
Ninety-five percent cannot.

00:51:04.390 --> 00:51:07.230
I mean, that's just-that's just stunning.

00:51:07.230 --> 00:51:11.579
But I gave you the number for the nation:
36 (percent)-I mean, 64 (percent) cannot.

00:51:11.579 --> 00:51:15.029
Nearly half the minority children in the country
are living in poverty today.

00:51:15.029 --> 00:51:16.609
Well, what's the way out?

00:51:16.609 --> 00:51:23.130
I mean, education has to be that ladder-has
to be.

00:51:23.130 --> 00:51:24.690
PATEL: Yes?

00:51:24.690 --> 00:51:27.599
Q: Beverly Lindsay, University College London.

00:51:27.599 --> 00:51:31.880
Governor, I actually have some follow ups
to what you were just stating, and particularly

00:51:31.880 --> 00:51:36.140
with reference to Michigan and Pennsylvania,
which I'm more familiar with.

00:51:36.140 --> 00:51:41.160
University of Michigan has some of the highest
tuitions in the country of public universities

00:51:41.160 --> 00:51:42.160
and so does Penn State.

00:51:42.160 --> 00:51:47.470
But at the same time, as you mentioned, there's
a need to train or retrain people who will

00:51:47.470 --> 00:51:49.799
be-who are displaced workers.

00:51:49.799 --> 00:51:53.690
Students are graduating with 30(,000 dollars),
40(,000 dollars), 50(,000 dollars), $75,000

00:51:53.690 --> 00:51:59.569
in debt from undergraduate degrees, 125,000
(dollars) or more with Ph.D. level degrees.

00:51:59.569 --> 00:52:05.249
How do you manage, or how do you balance the
educational needs in terms of methods of payments

00:52:05.249 --> 00:52:11.470
and return to those who need retraining versus
the 18- to 24-year-old traditional students

00:52:11.470 --> 00:52:16.990
or the 30- or 32-year-old earning the Ph.D.?
ENGLER: Well, you have to unbundle all of

00:52:16.990 --> 00:52:17.990
that.

00:52:17.990 --> 00:52:22.380
I've got a daughter who's going to be a senior
at Michigan next year, so I'm fully aware.

00:52:22.380 --> 00:52:27.249
I thought, you know, as a former governor,
maybe there should be something-(laughter)-break.

00:52:27.249 --> 00:52:31.010
My break is because I'm out here now, I get
to pay nonresident tuition-(laughter)-which

00:52:31.010 --> 00:52:35.069
is not very cheap at all.

00:52:35.069 --> 00:52:40.650
But I think you're-that the training, what
we-what we need to do is be very clear that

00:52:40.650 --> 00:52:42.299
not everybody needs to go to college, first
of all.

00:52:42.299 --> 00:52:46.680
And there's tremendous jobs in the economy
today.

00:52:46.680 --> 00:52:49.529
One of our staffers just in Iowa Falls runs
a company.

00:52:49.529 --> 00:52:55.309
Tom, you may know the guy, but they do a lot
of repair work on-they climb the poles for

00:52:55.309 --> 00:52:56.710
the electric wires.

00:52:56.710 --> 00:53:02.359
Those jobs, you-you know, high school degree,
and training, and you're making 65(,000 dollars)

00:53:02.359 --> 00:53:06.009
to $70,000 right away, almost.

00:53:06.009 --> 00:53:10.420
And when you get the kind of storms you'll
have, I mean, they can be making $8,000 on

00:53:10.420 --> 00:53:12.979
a weekend with the triple time.

00:53:12.979 --> 00:53:16.990
So, there's tremendous-there's lots of opportunities
out there.

00:53:16.990 --> 00:53:19.910
And I think one of the challenges is we've
got a lot of people that are ready to be trained

00:53:19.910 --> 00:53:21.140
to start at middle management.

00:53:21.140 --> 00:53:24.509
That's probably not a realistic aspiration.

00:53:24.509 --> 00:53:29.529
To the-to the cost, the other thing that I
think-this is where I think employers are

00:53:29.529 --> 00:53:35.269
starting to really think this through, is
that it's-I think hiring in the future is

00:53:35.269 --> 00:53:40.200
going to be much more about competencies than
it is about your degree, about what you studied,

00:53:40.200 --> 00:53:42.059
or where you studied at.

00:53:42.059 --> 00:53:45.289
And it's you look at me and said, can you
do this?

00:53:45.289 --> 00:53:47.550
And what standards?

00:53:47.550 --> 00:53:51.460
The American Welding Society's very clear
on what standards you need to follow to be

00:53:51.460 --> 00:53:54.690
a good welder and there's an exit test at
the end.

00:53:54.690 --> 00:53:58.589
You pass that, you can weld anywhere in the
country.

00:53:58.589 --> 00:54:03.859
That's not the case with a, you know, a degree
from some school somewhere.

00:54:03.859 --> 00:54:06.160
There's very different outcomes.

00:54:06.160 --> 00:54:09.900
Cyber is real hot today, so everybody's got
a cybersecurity program.

00:54:09.900 --> 00:54:15.609
But there's vast differences in what gets
taught in those programs and vast differences

00:54:15.609 --> 00:54:18.910
in the way they're valued once people understand.

00:54:18.910 --> 00:54:24.610
And we're working to bring transparency to
that so that a student-I think the worst thing

00:54:24.610 --> 00:54:29.130
we could do with any student, whether displaced
worker or young person, is have them go into

00:54:29.130 --> 00:54:33.759
a program because the advertisement is really
good and said, man, come here, do this, and

00:54:33.759 --> 00:54:39.180
you'll be that, only to find out the market
has a zero interest in that or doesn't value

00:54:39.180 --> 00:54:40.430
that program at that school.

00:54:40.430 --> 00:54:41.799
SHAMBAUGH: If I could just follow up on that.

00:54:41.799 --> 00:54:46.529
I think that's why some of the work that people
are trying to do here in terms of things like

00:54:46.529 --> 00:54:49.930
the college scorecard; to just try to get
more information into the hands of people

00:54:49.930 --> 00:54:54.059
if it-to the extent that education and retraining
have always been important but continue to

00:54:54.059 --> 00:54:57.220
be more important, to make sure that people
know, if they're going to take out a loan,

00:54:57.220 --> 00:54:59.989
that it's actually going to turn into a higher
income later on.

00:54:59.989 --> 00:55:03.239
And so, there are a lot-there's trying to
from Department of Ed-trying to get a lot

00:55:03.239 --> 00:55:05.140
more information out to people.

00:55:05.140 --> 00:55:09.469
And, for that matter, if there are-if you're
running programs like, say, a cybersecurity

00:55:09.469 --> 00:55:13.859
program that no one who graduates ever gets
a job and they never get anything from it,

00:55:13.859 --> 00:55:19.239
where you don't give them access to loan programs
in those-in those programs because you don't

00:55:19.239 --> 00:55:23.509
want some student borrowing a bunch of money
to go to a program and either not completing

00:55:23.509 --> 00:55:25.269
or not getting a job when they leave.

00:55:25.269 --> 00:55:28.559
Because that's who we're really worried about
are the people who are going to either poor

00:55:28.559 --> 00:55:30.509
programs or who don't graduate.

00:55:30.509 --> 00:55:34.180
And those are the people who really get hit
hard, and we need to take steps to protect

00:55:34.180 --> 00:55:35.180
them.

00:55:35.180 --> 00:55:37.779
And we're trying to.

00:55:37.779 --> 00:55:41.390
PATEL: Yes, in the back.

00:55:41.390 --> 00:55:45.810
Q: Governor Engler, Jack Janes from Johns
Hopkins.

00:55:45.810 --> 00:55:48.579
A couple of years ago, we did a program on
the German apprenticeship program.

00:55:48.579 --> 00:55:52.969
And I know Mr. Rattner's also familiar with
Germany; I was actually going to direct my

00:55:52.969 --> 00:55:54.559
question to Mr. Shambaugh.

00:55:54.559 --> 00:55:58.500
There is a system in place in Germany that
does pretty well here.

00:55:58.500 --> 00:56:03.339
And one of the key factors is that a company
like Opel or Siemens is training of thousands

00:56:03.339 --> 00:56:05.869
of people regardless of where they flow around
the country later on.

00:56:05.869 --> 00:56:09.789
I'm not sure that is the case with many American
employers here.

00:56:09.789 --> 00:56:13.809
What are you doing, looking around in the
White House, to see where people have a successful

00:56:13.809 --> 00:56:19.161
formula here and possibly being able to adopt
it in the United States?

00:56:19.161 --> 00:56:23.609
SHAMBAUGH: So, I think, frankly, we spend
a lot of time looking around the world at-to

00:56:23.609 --> 00:56:25.009
try to see what has worked.

00:56:25.009 --> 00:56:30.099
I actually remember back when I was at CEA
in 2009 and 2010 spending a lot of time looking

00:56:30.099 --> 00:56:35.200
at what choices Germany had made in terms
of how they-what was happening with their

00:56:35.200 --> 00:56:36.200
workers.

00:56:36.200 --> 00:56:41.700
I do think, you know, this is one place where
I think U.S., you know-every education system

00:56:41.700 --> 00:56:42.700
is different.

00:56:42.700 --> 00:56:47.249
The U.S. system is, so trying to entirely
transplant a system from country to another

00:56:47.249 --> 00:56:48.249
may not work.

00:56:48.249 --> 00:56:52.430
But I think it's one where at least many people,
myself included, think U.S. community colleges

00:56:52.430 --> 00:56:57.510
can fill a similar role to what, or at least
a parallel role to what you see in Germany

00:56:57.510 --> 00:56:58.510
in some cases.

00:56:58.510 --> 00:57:01.930
I think it is, honestly, why you saw-I don't
know if it's been one or two years ago-the

00:57:01.930 --> 00:57:08.219
president proposing to make community college
free, and to try to-and we continue to try-both

00:57:08.219 --> 00:57:11.640
the vice president and his wife have been
very involved trying to get involved with

00:57:11.640 --> 00:57:16.380
the community colleges, have them partner
with firms and companies in the area to try

00:57:16.380 --> 00:57:19.999
to make sure that you've got people, A, able
to get into these programs.

00:57:19.999 --> 00:57:21.940
It may not take them to a four-year degree.

00:57:21.940 --> 00:57:23.530
It may not be what they want.

00:57:23.530 --> 00:57:26.109
Or it may give them the avenue to one if it
is what they want.

00:57:26.109 --> 00:57:28.940
But it will also open up a lot of opportunities
for training.

00:57:28.940 --> 00:57:30.641
So, I think there is a lot of attention on
that.

00:57:30.641 --> 00:57:34.839
And I think there's a lot of scope to do more
here, and that's why it's been in proposals.

00:57:34.839 --> 00:57:36.809
They haven't passed.

00:57:36.809 --> 00:57:37.900
But I think it would be helpful.

00:57:37.900 --> 00:57:43.039
RATTNER: I'll just add an anecdote to buttress
the point, but not necessarily solve the problem.

00:57:43.039 --> 00:57:48.539
But when Volkswagen opened a plant in Chattanooga
about five or six years ago-2,000 jobs-they

00:57:48.539 --> 00:57:52.599
got thousands of applicants even though the
jobs were only paying at the time $14 1/2

00:57:52.599 --> 00:57:56.400
an hour, which is a-which is a working class
wage, not a middle class wage.

00:57:56.400 --> 00:57:58.989
But what Volkswagen found was that the workers
really weren't qualified.

00:57:58.989 --> 00:58:02.249
They really couldn't do the job, and they
didn't even-and nor could they even necessarily

00:58:02.249 --> 00:58:03.859
find the people to train them to do the job.

00:58:03.859 --> 00:58:07.979
So, the actually ended up bringing people
from Germany to come over and train them to

00:58:07.979 --> 00:58:11.349
do the job, which I think illustrates the
problem, although, as I said, doesn't provide

00:58:11.349 --> 00:58:12.349
necessarily a solution.

00:58:12.349 --> 00:58:13.349
PATEL: Right.

00:58:13.349 --> 00:58:14.809
ENGLER: Yeah, see, now that was exactly the
part I was going to build on, that one of

00:58:14.809 --> 00:58:21.999
the things that U.S. business has got to do
a far better job of is helping to explain

00:58:21.999 --> 00:58:26.150
to the trainers, to the educators what it
is we need.

00:58:26.150 --> 00:58:28.790
And they have to do a better job of listening
to that.

00:58:28.790 --> 00:58:34.150
And there's plenty to go around here on both
sides of not figuring this out.

00:58:34.150 --> 00:58:39.349
We do know that for governors today in recruiting
a plant to come to their state, more important

00:58:39.349 --> 00:58:44.829
than tax incentives or, you know, maybe not
the new road or the railroad siding, but right

00:58:44.829 --> 00:58:49.759
up there with that is how-who's going to work
here, who's going to train them?

00:58:49.759 --> 00:58:53.890
And I don't-I don't think there's a deal that's
done where it doesn't include the training

00:58:53.890 --> 00:58:55.359
program right alongside.

00:58:55.359 --> 00:59:00.979
And the training program in those cases are
tell us what you need, and we'll train the

00:59:00.979 --> 00:59:03.740
workers for you.

00:59:03.740 --> 00:59:09.539
When Governor Perry was trying to lure Boeing,
he thought they were-they were possibly willing

00:59:09.539 --> 00:59:11.460
to move to Texas.

00:59:11.460 --> 00:59:16.930
Well, to try to compete, he even went so far-and
I thought this was highly creative-he said,

00:59:16.930 --> 00:59:21.900
which professors in which universities, because
some of their jobs are very high tech, in

00:59:21.900 --> 00:59:25.299
the materials area matter most?

00:59:25.299 --> 00:59:30.509
We'll try to bring those professors to this
university so that we can set up the higher

00:59:30.509 --> 00:59:33.690
level programs to go right along with the
training programs.

00:59:33.690 --> 00:59:35.349
Very creative strategy.

00:59:35.349 --> 00:59:40.660
They didn't win the deal, but I had not heard
of that done and I was envious I hadn't thought

00:59:40.660 --> 00:59:41.660
of it.

00:59:41.660 --> 00:59:42.660
(Laughter.)

00:59:42.660 --> 00:59:43.910
PATEL: Well, and with that, we will leave
that at the last word.

00:59:43.910 --> 00:59:49.359
Thank you so much to our panelists for the
discussion and to all of you for your questions.

00:59:49.359 --> 00:59:50.359
(Applause.)

00:59:50.359 --> 00:59:50.360
(END)

